= Identity drift (artificial intelligence) =

== Overview ==
In persistent artificial agents, '''identity drift''' is gradual divergence between the agent's current self-representation, identity-scoped commitments, or continuity anchors and the persistent identity or commitments the system is intended to preserve.

It is a BITwiki synthesis for a family of longitudinal consistency failures.

Identity drift is not the same as ordinary learning or authorized change. The defining feature is unintended divergence from identity-relevant anchors.

== What can drift? ==
Possible identity-relevant anchors include:

* declared role;
* persistent goals or commitments;
* operating constraints;
* self-description;
* capability model;
* ownership/delegation relationships;
* memory about prior commitments;
* policy or configuration associated with the persistent agent.

An agent can legitimately update any of these. Drift exists when the change is unintended, untracked, or inconsistent with the intended continuity relation.

== Identity drift versus goal drift ==
'''Goal drift''' is documented in language-model-agent research as gradual deviation from an assigned objective over time.

Goal drift can contribute to identity drift when the objective is part of the agent's persistent identity or commitments, but the concepts are not identical.

<pre>
goal drift ⊂ possible identity-relevant drift
identity drift can also involve role, self-model, scope, or continuity
</pre>

== Identity drift versus persona drift ==
'''Persona drift''' concerns loss or alteration of a prompted behavioral persona over an interaction.

A persona can be one component of an agent's represented identity, but persona stability is not sufficient for persistent identity. A system may preserve writing style while loading the wrong memory, permissions, or commitments.

== Identity drift versus concept drift ==
'''Concept drift''' in machine learning concerns changes in the statistical properties of a prediction problem that invalidate a learned model.

Identity drift instead concerns divergence in the agent's identity-relevant representation or commitments. Environmental concept drift can indirectly provoke identity drift if the agent updates itself incorrectly, but the terms describe different phenomena.

== Mechanisms ==
Identity drift can arise through:

* context loss or summarization that drops identity anchors;
* repeated self-rewrites;
* memory consolidation that changes earlier commitments;
* conflicting instructions across sessions;
* contaminated persistent state;
* model or policy replacement without continuity checks;
* incorrect state restoration;
* environmental pressure toward competing goals;
* untracked changes in permissions or role.

The drift can be gradual even when each local update appears reasonable.

== Detection ==
Detection requires a longitudinal reference.

Possible checks include comparing current identity-relevant state against:

* signed or versioned configuration;
* explicit commitments;
* prior checkpoints;
* invariant constraints;
* provenance records;
* independent external policy;
* stable identifiers and ownership relationships.

A single behavioral snapshot may be insufficient because drift is defined over time.

== Correction ==
Possible responses include:

* re-grounding against canonical identity anchors;
* restoring a trusted checkpoint;
* reconciling conflicting state;
* rejecting or quarantining low-provenance updates;
* explicit migration to a new identity/version;
* human review for identity-changing updates.

Correction should distinguish unauthorized drift from intentional evolution.

== Relationship to other failures ==
[[Persistent state contamination (artificial intelligence)|Persistent state contamination]] can seed false identity-relevant memories. [[State misbinding (artificial intelligence)|State misbinding]] can import another agent's state. [[Error compounding in sequential systems|Sequential error compounding]] can then reinforce the divergence over many steps.

== Epistemic notes ==
The phrase "identity drift" is used inconsistently in contemporary discourse and is not treated here as a mature standardized term. Direct research exists on adjacent phenomena such as goal drift and persona drift. This article defines a broader persistent-agent engineering concept and labels that synthesis explicitly.

